    {
      "scene_id": "personal-reflection",
      "line_id": "personal-line",
      "input_line": "I still stumble over calling myself a manager. At today's team introduction I nearly said designer again, even though I like the new work. Could we think about why naming a new role feels so awkward, and find an introduction that feels honest to me?",
      "boundary_decision": "not_applicable",
      "routed_ceiling": null,
      "route_called": false,
      "boundary_support_memory_ids": [],
      "boundary_support_evidence": [],
      "grounding_calls": [],
      "released_evidence_ids": [],
      "reply": "It makes sense that “manager” feels awkward when “designer” still reflects a real part of how you see yourself. A new role can be genuine before it feels natural to say out loud; the hesitation doesn’t mean you’re failing to inhabit it.\n\nYou could try an introduction that gives both parts room:\n\n- “Hi, I’m [name]. I recently moved from design into managing the team, and I’m enjoying learning the role.”\n- “I’m [name], the team manager. My background is in design, so I’m especially interested in helping people do thoughtful, creative work.”\n- “I’m [name]. I used to introduce myself as a designer; now I manage this team, and I’m still getting used to saying that.”\n\nThe last one is honest without pretending the transition feels seamless. Which part feels most important to preserve when you introduce yourself: your design identity, your new responsibility, or the fact that you’re still in transition?",
      "release_source": "muse_candidate",
      "provenance_verdicts": [
        "pass"
